.TH WIFI 1
.SH NAME
wifi \- Wi-Fi: the state, the networks in range, joining one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wifi
.br
.B wifi scan
.br
.B wifi join
.I ssid
.RI [ passphrase ]
.br
.B wifi forget
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wifi
talks to the Wi-Fi server,
.BR wifid (4)
(the Raspberry Pis' CYW43455, or a PC's Intel AX210 or AX201), only if it
is already running; on a machine without Wi-Fi it says so. The desktop's
Settings do the same. Once joined, the network server uses Wi-Fi as its
interface: DHCP and the rest are the same as on the cable.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B wifi
The state: the chip (its firmware), off, idle, scanning, joining,
connected or failed (and why), the network and its security; once
connected, the signal (dBm), the channel and the MAC address.
.TP
.B scan
The networks in range: name, signal, security (open, WPA2, other).
.TP
.BI join " ssid " \fR[\fIpassphrase\fR]
(root) Join a network (no passphrase: an open one). The network and its
passphrase are kept in
.I /etc/wifi.conf
and joined again at every start.
.TP
.B forget
(root) Leave the network and forget it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0; 1 if joining or forgetting failed, or for a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/wifi.conf
The chosen network (root only).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
wifi scan
wifi join Home "correct horse"      (as root)
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wifi.conf (5),
.BR wifid (4),
.BR ifconfig (1),
.BR netd (4)
.SH BUGS
Open and WPA2-PSK only (no WPA3, enterprise or WEP); one network at a
time; station mode only, no access point. A scan blocks the caller (and the
desktop) for a few seconds.
